WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.950
- [Narrator] There's a creature scurrying

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:06.950
across your face right now. Yes, you,

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.210
and at some point, maybe
now, maybe in a few days,

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:16.100
it's going to find a nice
cozy pore in your skin

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.023
and lay a single enormous egg.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.140
Meet the face mites.
They're smaller than a grain

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.750
of sand, are a kind of
arachnid like spiders,

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:33.100
and they feast on the oil
and cells in your skin,

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:37.380
particularly on your oily
nose, cheeks, and forehead.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.750
Scientists suspect
they've been living on us

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:44.260
since the dawn of humanity
over 200,000 years ago,

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.010
and today, studies suggest
practically every adult

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:51.170
on the planet has thousands of them.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:55.290
Odds are you've been living
with them your whole life.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.500
Babies quickly get them from their parents

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.780
a few days after birth,
and once those face mites

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.650
are on you, the only
thing they enjoy as much

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:07.450
as slurping oil and
nutrients from your pores is

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:11.730
having sex all over your face.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.120
Afterwards, females burrow deep

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.680
into your pores where they lay their eggs.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.869
The eggs end up in one of two places

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:21.690
depending on the species of face mite.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.820
The first species, called
Demodex folliculorum,

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.640
lays its eggs in your hair follicle,

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.860
while the second prefers nesting
in your sebaceous glands.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.210
And in under two weeks, the babies hatch,

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.940
mate, lay their own eggs and die,

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:39.673
leaving behind a pile
of decomposing corpses.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.870
Now, you can wash some of this off,

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.697
but you'll never eradicate them completely

00:01:46.697 --> 00:01:49.450
because even if you treat
them with antibiotics,

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:52.448
they'll return in about six weeks, tops.

00:01:52.448 --> 00:01:55.080
You'll pick them up from towels, pillows,

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.950
and your loved ones.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.770
Well, that all sounds horrific,

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:03.330
but usually face mites are harmless.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:05.610
They only become a
problem when they multiply

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.105
out of control. This can happen in people

00:02:08.105 --> 00:02:10.830
with an impaired immune system.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.400
It's also been seen in
people with a painful skin

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.953
condition called rosacea.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.430
Normally, you'll have
around one or two mites

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:20.930
per square centimeter of skin,

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.300
but one study found
that people with rosacea

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.480
had 10 times the normal amount.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.680
Believe it or not, in some cases,

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.990
face mites can be useful.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.780
Researchers can actually
study your face mites

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.560
to learn about your ancestors.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.883
You see, most mites often
stay within a community.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:44.240
So over time, they've evolved
into distinct lineages

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.340
in different geographic
regions, and by comparing

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.670
their DNA, scientists can
trace how different groups

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.470
of humans migrated across the world.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:56.640
For example, a study
found that European mites

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.270
genetically diverged from East Asian mites

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:02.000
around 40,000 years ago.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.050
That's the same time European
and East Asian humans

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:08.210
parted ways. Pretty handy.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:11.500
So when it comes down to
bugs crawling all over you,

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.293
it could be a lot worse.

